          lemma: "ὑποκριταί … ἀπέχουσιν",
          title: "Hypocrites, and a receipt marked “paid”",
          body: "Two words with their paint still wet. **Ὑποκριτής** was not yet a moral term — it was the ordinary word for a *stage actor*, the man behind the mask in the theatre (there was a fine Greek one a few miles from Nazareth, at Sepphoris). Jesus seems to be the first to turn it on religion: piety performed for an audience is *acting*, and the audience's applause is the whole wage. Which is what the second word says: **ἀπέχουσιν** is bookkeeping jargon, the verb stamped on receipts — *paid in full, account settled*. The satire is precise: the trumpeted donor is not cheated, he is *paid* — in the currency he chose. The chapter's triple refrain (alms, prayer, fasting) is not against public goodness (5:16 commanded visible light) but against goodness whose real address is the spectators; the test of the difference, three times over, is what you still do in secret.",
