      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a6.11.1',
          kind: "translation",
          refs: ['g6.11.5'],
          lemma: "ἐπιούσιος",
          title: "Epiousios — the word that exists nowhere else",
          body: "**Ἐπιούσιος**, traditionally “daily,” occurs here and in Luke's parallel — and *nowhere else in all surviving Greek*. Origen, the third century's best-read scholar, said the evangelists apparently coined it; one alleged occurrence on an Egyptian shopping list has since gone missing and is disputed. So every translation is an educated guess among etymologies: bread *for the coming day* (ἡ ἐπιοῦσα, tomorrow's ration prayed for tonight — a day-labourer's petition), bread *for existence* (ἐπί + οὐσία, what subsistence needs), or the old liturgical *supersubstantial* bread (Jerome's Vulgate at this verse). We render “bread for the day ahead” — the labourer's sense, which fits the next petition-block's horizon (v. 34: tomorrow can worry about itself). It is worth knowing that the most-recited sentence in Christianity carries at its centre a word no one can fully parse — the prayer has an untranslatable spot, and two millennia of praying it have not required its resolution.",
        },
      ],
