      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a6.24.1',
          kind: "translation",
          refs: ['g6.24.27'],
          lemma: "μαμωνᾶς",
          title: "Mammon — money, left standing as a rival god",
          body: "**Μαμωνᾶς** transliterates Aramaic *māmônā* — wealth, property, that-in-which-one-trusts (likely from the same root as *amen*: what is firm). It is not the name of a demon in any ancient source; medieval imagination promoted it to one. But notice what the sentence does grammatically: it sets Mammon **parallel to God** as a possible *master* — δουλεύειν is a slave's service, exclusive by definition. Money is the only rival that gets this treatment anywhere in Jesus' teaching: not sex, not power, not Rome — wealth alone is personified as a competing deity, presumably because it is the one that most successfully impersonates providence. It answers prayers, promises security, rewards devotion. We keep the transliteration, as Matthew did, precisely so it sounds like what the sentence says it is: a god's name. The choice offered is not between God and *having* money, but between two objects of ultimate trust — the verse's economics are less about the wallet than about where the nerves run for safety.",
        },
      ],
