      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a6.25.1',
          kind: "translation",
          refs: ['g6.25.6'],
          lemma: "μεριμνάω",
          title: "“Anxious” — the divided mind, not the prudent one",
          body: "**Μεριμνάω** — six times in ten verses, the passage's drumbeat — is not “plan,” “work,” or “save”; the birds are fed, but they still forage, and Jesus' hearers were farmers who would sow the next morning. The word names the *churning* — care that divides (its likely root, μερίζω, means to split) and fragments the self across imagined futures; Paul uses it for being “pulled apart” by concerns. The passage is thus not economic advice, still less a rebuke to the poor (spoken, note, to people who genuinely did not know about tomorrow's bread — its promise must be heard alongside the prayer *give us today our bread*, and the command to be the answer to that prayer for each other). It is a diagnosis: anxiety is treasure stored in the future, where the heart then goes to live. The prescribed dose is v. 34, perhaps the most practical sentence in the Sermon: **tomorrow can be anxious for itself; today's trouble is enough for today** — a one-day-at-a-time rule two millennia before it became the motto of recovery movements.",
        },
      ],
